sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: a task starts as a copy of its Project's default Instance, a clone `task-<id>` that the task owns (nested-fleet Projects since 2026-11-13).
  %% Nick 2026-11-14: every Project whose default Instance is a container gets task groups as copies too; a plain clone, with no inner fleet.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (step 5): task VMs are removed. A Project whose default is no container is refused by name; the VM claim and its queue are gone.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (task small fixes): the CLI waits for an active copy by default, as instance:create --wait-active; the API stays asynchronous.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (task 5634): a failed create no longer ends the task: it goes back to todo, the create starts once more after the copy is gone, and a second failure asks for assistance.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:start
  %% kind: tasks.provision
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  Note over GW: Decide where the task runs<br/>copy or refusal
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `InstancePlacement::taskCopy` decides once: a Project with `nested_fleet` on, or whose default Instance is a container, starts the task as a copy of that default; any other Project is refused
  %%> - no other step decides it; later steps follow the group's own workspace
  alt the Project's tasks run as copies
    Note over GW: Admit the copy's create<br/>instance.create · task-TASK
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - the task is in todo and holds no workspace; otherwise `tasks.not_todo` or `tasks.group_closed`
    %%> - the Project's default Instance is active, with a seed snapshot, and runs or sleeps; otherwise HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_source_unavailable`, also for a default stopped by hand, and nothing is recorded
    %%> - before the transaction, one bounded read of the default's container (at most about 6 s) confirms it runs or sleeps: its power record can be stale after a stop by hand; a container that does not run and has no asleep marker, or that cannot be read, refuses the same way
    %%> - an asleep default stays asleep: the copy clones its seed snapshot, which needs no running container
    %%> - no Instance is named `task-TASK` yet; otherwise `tasks.copy_name_taken`
    %%> - the task holds no copy of a failed create that Orbit still removes; otherwise `tasks.copy_removing`
    %%> - for a nested-fleet Project only: the default's Node holds fewer nested-fleet task copies than the Gateway's `ORBIT_TASKS_COPIES_MAX` (default 1); every nested copy that a task owns counts, also one that fails or is being destroyed; otherwise `tasks.copy_capacity_full`, with no wait. A copy of any other Project is a plain clone: it neither counts nor waits
    %%> - before the transaction, one bounded read of the Node's ID ranges (at most about 4 s): root's subordinate range and every container's ID map, stopped ones too; an unreadable Node refuses, `tasks.copy_id_ranges_unreadable`
    %%> - with the copy placed as Incus places ranges, a free isolated ID range stays for the next seed of every default on the Node; a container that an open create admitted counts as held; otherwise `tasks.copy_id_range_full`, with no wait, also for a plain clone
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one transaction: the clone `task-TASK` on branch `task-TASK`, owned by the task and bound to it, the task `reserved`, and the `instance.create` Operation that the API returns at once
    %%> - the CLI follows that create and then its completion, `result.completion_operation_id`, and reads the copy: it exits 0 only when the copy is `active`, as `instance:create --wait-active` does. `--no-wait` ends at the create. An API caller polls the same: the create, the completion, then the Instance's `status`
    %%> - Swamp starts the run only after that commit: a Gateway that stops before it leaves no record and no run
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the create runs as every clone's does: the copy of the default's seed, its own database, Route and Processes, its inner fleet when the Project has one, its T3 server registered with Conn (the container create chart)
    %%> - the copy presents itself as `task-<task id>.<project slug>`: its T3 server's name, and its hostname when the slug is a lowercase DNS label and the name fits 63 characters, else the long form; its Incus name stays `instance-<id>`
    %%> - the copy's agent gets the fixed task model key, as every clone does: it is not per task
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-create
    alt the create succeeds
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>thread usable
      Note over GW: Keep the task reserved<br/>the completion runs
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - a succeeded create means the copy's thread is usable: its T3 server is registered; the copy stays `source_resolved` and its `instance.complete` sets up the database, the Processes and a nested copy's inner fleet
      %%> - while the completion runs, a turn, a check, a publish and a proof answer `tasks.copy_not_ready`
      alt the completion succeeds
        SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>copy active
        Note over GW: Run the task<br/>running
        %%> When:
        %%> - the completion succeeded and the copy is `active`: fully set up; a nested copy's fleet may still be starting (`instance.fleet`, see `fleet_ready_at`), and a proof waits for it
        %%> - after a retried completion, the task also drops the assistance that the failed completion recorded, and only that request
      else the completion fails
        SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
        Note over GW: Keep the copy<br/>ask for assistance
        %%> On failure:
        %%> - every end of the completion but success and an expired run budget counts: a failed or interrupted run, a completion the create could not admit, a cancel
        %%> - an agent may already have worked in the copy, so Orbit removes nothing: no destroy is queued
        %%> - the task stays `reserved` and keeps its copy; `task:cancel` still works, with its read of the copy's work
        %%> - the task records a failure assistance request, unless a direction request is open: it names the completion Operation, its failed step, its error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`; `task:show` and `task:status` show it
        %%> - a turn, a check, a publish and a proof of the task answer HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_completion_failed`, with `operation_id`, `failed_step`, `error_code` and `retry` in the details: the copy's Processes and inner fleet did not start, and a retry may run at the same time
        %%> - `task:start` keeps refusing the reserved task; the retry is `orbit instance:complete ID`, and while it runs a turn, a check, a publish and a proof answer `tasks.copy_not_ready`
        %%> - an expired budget changes nothing: the run may still be stopping, so the task stays `reserved` with its copy
      end
    else the create fails
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
      Note over GW: Task back to todo<br/>remove copy, retry once
      %%> On failure:
      %%> - every end of the create but success, a cancel and an expired run budget counts: a failed run, a failed register or baseline read, a start that Swamp refuses
      %%> - the task goes back to `todo` and still owns the copy until its destroy; a queued job removes the copy with `instance:destroy` with force
      %%> - the first failure is recorded on the task: its Operation, failed step and error code; when the destroy succeeds, Orbit starts the create once more for the same task, as its first caller
      %%> - the retry's failure, or a retry that the start refuses, leaves the task in `todo` with no workspace and records a failure assistance request that names both Operations, their failed steps and their error codes; `task:show` shows it; no third create starts
      %%> - `task:start` of that task works once the copy is gone; while it is still bound, `tasks.copy_removing`; a copy that comes up `active` clears that request
      %%> - a cancel of the create, and the create of a proof's copy, fail the task as before: nothing starts again
      %%> - `task:cancel` of the `todo` task while it holds the failed copy cancels it when the copy's destroy succeeds; a task moved to `backlog` before then owes no retry
      %%> - the job destroys at once only when the records prove that no agent could have worked in the copy: the create did not succeed and no T3 server is registered for it
      %%> - otherwise it first reads the copy's work as `task:cancel` does and destroys only a clean copy; a copy with work origin lacks, or a read that does not answer, stays, and the task records a `removal_failure` assistance request with the unpushed, uncommitted, untracked and stash counts, or the read's reason, and `orbit task:cancel TASK`, with `--discard` if the work is not wanted
      %%> - an expired budget changes nothing: the run may still be stopping, so the task stays `reserved` with its copy
      %%> - while the task keeps the copy, `task:cancel` admits its destroy, and `orbit instance:destroy ID --yes --force` does too
    end
  else the Project's default is no container
    Note over GW: Refuse by name<br/>tasks.vm_runtime_removed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - HTTP 422 `tasks.vm_runtime_removed`: task VMs are removed, and a task runs only in a copy of its Project's default container Instance
    %%> - the message says what to do: move a checkout default into a container with instance:migrate, or create a container default with instance:create, then start the task and work in its copy
    %%> - nothing is recorded: the task stays `todo` with no workspace, and no Operation, VM, identity or wait exists
    %%> - every task VM command answers the same refusal first: a turn of a group without a copy, package and script; prep, prep-fix, build and review round are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md#the-pi-task-loop)
  end
